import{_ as s,o as i,c as t,ao as a}from"./chunks/framework.CuFqzND2.js";const k=JSON.parse('{"title":"MPI scaling","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"mpi.md","filePath":"mpi.md","lastUpdated":null}'),n={name:"mpi.md"};function o(h,e,l,d,r,c){return i(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[a(`<h1 id="MPI-scaling" tabindex="-1">MPI scaling <a class="header-anchor" href="#MPI-scaling" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;MPI scaling {#MPI-scaling}&quot;">​</a></h1><p><a href="https://github.com/WaterLily-jl/WaterLily-Benchmarks/tree/main/mpi" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>mpi/</code></a> benchmarks the MPI extension of WaterLily (branch <a href="https://github.com/WaterLily-jl/WaterLily.jl/pull/281" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>mpi-igg</code></a>, on top of <a href="https://github.com/eth-cscs/ImplicitGlobalGrid.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">ImplicitGlobalGrid.jl</a>) on CPUs and GPUs: strong and weak scaling, and correctness checks against a run without MPI. It is separate from <code>benchmark.sh</code>: each run is one MPI launch of <code>mpi/scaling.jl</code>, which prints one line per run instead of writing JSON files.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">File</th><th style="text-align:right;">What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>mpi/setup.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">builds the <code>mpi/</code> environment (or another one, <code>--env</code>): WaterLily from <code>$WATERLILY_DIR</code>, MPI.jl bound to the system MPI, the WaterLily backend</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>mpi/scaling.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">times <code>sim_step!</code> of a case on every rank and prints the time per step; <code>--check</code> also records global diagnostics</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>mpi/check.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">compares the diagnostics of several runs with a reference run</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>mpi/cuda_mpi_test.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">checks the CUDA-aware MPI alone: correctness on device buffers, and the ping-pong speed of device against host buffers (profile it with Nsight Systems to see how MPI moves device buffers)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>mpi/halo_profile.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">profiles ImplicitGlobalGrid&#39;s <code>update_halo!</code> of WaterLily-sized device arrays alone: time per call, the CUDA calls and kernels behind it (<code>CUDA.@profile</code>), and where the host spends the time (Julia&#39;s profiler); optionally without CUDA.jl&#39;s implicit synchronization between streams, or with the routine of WaterLily&#39;s <code>face_comm!</code> (all split dimensions at once, as in the Poisson iterations since WaterLily <code>12c339c</code>)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>mpi/halo_trace.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">times each phase of ImplicitGlobalGrid&#39;s <code>update_halo!</code> (packing, posting, waiting, unpacking), per dimension and rank, next to the bare MPI exchange of the same faces from plain, page-locked and device buffers; needs the timers of the ImplicitGlobalGrid fork (branch <a href="https://github.com/b-fg/ImplicitGlobalGrid.jl/tree/halo-trace" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>halo-trace</code></a>) and <code>IGG_TRACE=1</code>, which also make <code>scaling.jl</code> print the time per step spent in <code>update_halo!</code>, by array size</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>mpi/plot_strong.jl</code>, <code>mpi/plot_weak.jl</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">plot the scaling curves from the job outputs (with the <code>plotting</code> environment)</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>mpi/jobs/</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">the environment and job scripts of each cluster (<code>jupiter_*</code>, <code>snellius_*</code>) and <code>submit.sh</code></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Setup" tabindex="-1">Setup <a class="header-anchor" href="#Setup" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Setup {#Setup}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On a login node (it needs internet), with a WaterLily.jl clone on branch <code>mpi-igg</code>:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">source</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/jobs/jupiter_env.sh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                                # the modules of the jobs, and WATERLILY_DIR</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/setup.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --cuda-local</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                               # GPUs: KernelAbstractions backend, CUDA.jl on the CUDA module</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/setup.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --env=mpi/cpu</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --backend=SIMD</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --cuda-local</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">  # CPUs, one thread per rank: a second environment</span></span></code></pre></div><p><code>jupiter_env.sh</code> expects Julia from juliaup in <code>PATH</code>, loads GCC, ParaStationMPI (or <code>$WATERLILY_MPI</code>, e.g. <code>OpenMPI</code>) and CUDA, sets <code>JULIA_CUDA_MEMORY_POOL=none</code> (CUDA-aware MPI cannot pass memory of CUDA.jl&#39;s pool, see the known issues of MPI.jl), and keeps <code>JULIA_DEPOT_PATH</code> and <code>WATERLILY_DIR</code> when they are set (by default both go under <code>$PROJECT/$USER</code>). On a CPU cluster use <code>julia mpi/setup.jl --backend=SIMD</code> (one thread per rank). <code>setup.jl</code> binds MPI.jl to the MPI library of the loaded module, so run it again whenever the modules change (the preferences go to <code>mpi/LocalPreferences.toml</code>, not tracked; Julia 1.12 and later also write the path of WaterLily into <code>mpi/Project.toml</code>, a local change not to commit); MPIPreferences does not recognise ParaStation MPI, so <code>setup.jl</code> gives it the MPICH ABI (<code>--mpi-abi</code> sets another). On a laptop without an MPI module, <code>--system-mpi=false</code> keeps MPI.jl&#39;s own MPICH. <code>--cuda-local</code> sets CUDA.jl&#39;s runtime preference before anything loads CUDA.jl: loaded with its own CUDA libraries first, CUDA.jl mixes them with those of the CUDA module. The backend preference is fixed per environment: GPU runs need <code>KernelAbstractions</code>, and <code>scaling.jl</code> stops otherwise. So the CPU runs on JUPITER, one thread per rank as on Snellius, have their own environment, <code>mpi/cpu</code> (not tracked), with the packages of <code>mpi/Project.toml</code> and the <code>SIMD</code> backend; <code>--cuda-local</code> there keeps CUDA.jl, which it also holds, on the libraries of the CUDA module.</p><h2 id="Running-scaling.jl" tabindex="-1">Running <code>scaling.jl</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#Running-scaling.jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Running \`scaling.jl\` {#Running-scaling.jl}&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=mpi</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/scaling.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --case=tgv</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --log2p=7</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                               # serial</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">mpiexec</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -n</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 4</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=mpi</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/scaling.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --case=tgv</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --log2p=7</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">                  # 4 CPU ranks</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">srun</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -n</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 8</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=mpi</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/scaling.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --case=tgv</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --log2p=8</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --backend=CuArray</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">   # 8 GPUs</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The cases are those of <code>src/cases.jl</code>: <code>tgv</code> is <code>tgv_periodic</code> (triply periodic, a <code>(2^p)^3</code> box) and <code>sphere</code> is the sphere with a convective exit (a <code>16D×6D×6D</code> box, <code>D = 2^p</code>). <code>--log2p</code> sets the global size; with <code>--weak</code> it is the size per rank, and the global grid grows with the number of ranks (each rank of the weak TGV holds one period). <code>--dims=a,b,c</code> gives the size in cells instead. Other options: <code>--backend=Array|CuArray</code>, <code>--T=Float32|Float64</code>, <code>--steps</code> and <code>--warmup</code> (timed and untimed steps), and <code>--serial</code> (no MPI, even under <code>srun</code>). Rank 0 prints</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>[ Info: MPI np=8  case=tgv  grid=256x256x256  local=128x128x128  100 steps  per_step=...ms  levels=7  mean_iters=1.0  cost=...ns/DOF/dt  backend=CuArray</span></span></code></pre></div><p>after a header with the commits of WaterLily and of WaterLily-Benchmarks, the MPI library and the device of each rank. When every rank sees all the GPUs of its node, ImplicitGlobalGrid gives each node-local rank its own GPU; when the launcher binds one GPU per rank (Slurm <code>--gpus-per-task=1</code>, the default on JUPITER), each rank keeps the one it sees. Check in the header that the ranks of a node show different GPUs. To test the GPU code paths on a machine with one GPU, let several ranks share it: <code>CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0 mpiexec -n 2 ...</code> (the timings are then meaningless).</p><h2 id="Correctness-of-the-multi-GPU-TGV" tabindex="-1">Correctness of the multi-GPU TGV <a class="header-anchor" href="#Correctness-of-the-multi-GPU-TGV" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Correctness of the multi-GPU TGV {#Correctness-of-the-multi-GPU-TGV}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>--check=k</code> records the global kinetic energy, enstrophy, <code>max|u|</code>, <code>max|p|</code>, time step and Poisson iterations every <code>k</code> steps, and <code>--out=file.csv</code> saves them. <a href="https://github.com/WaterLily-jl/WaterLily-Benchmarks/tree/main/mpi/jobs/jupiter_tgv_check.sh" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>mpi/jobs/jupiter_tgv_check.sh</code></a> runs the TGV from its initial condition on one GPU without MPI and with MPI on 1, 4 and 8 GPUs (two nodes), on 8 GPUs with CUDA-aware halos, and on 8 CPU ranks, then compares them all with <code>mpi/check.jl</code>:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">sbatch</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -A</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">budge</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">t</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/jobs/jupiter_tgv_check.sh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 7</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 200</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 10</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">   # log2p, steps, record every 10 steps</span></span></code></pre></div><p>What to expect, from the same check of a <code>64^3</code> TGV over 100 steps on one laptop GPU shared by up to 4 ranks:</p><ul><li><p>MPI on one GPU is identical to the run without MPI (<code>identical</code> in the table), as on CPUs.</p></li><li><p>CUDA-aware (<code>IGG_CUDAAWARE_MPI=1</code>) and host-staged (<code>=0</code>) halos give identical results: only the transport changes.</p></li><li><p>With more ranks, the diagnostics differ slightly from the serial run (largest relative difference of the kinetic energy about <code>4e-4</code> on 2 and 4 ranks): the halo is one cell wide, so at the faces between ranks the convection term uses the one-sided form of the domain boundaries.</p></li><li><p>GPU and CPU runs with the same decomposition differ by FP32 round-off (about <code>3e-5</code>).</p></li></ul><p>The halos of the solver (velocity, pressure, multigrid levels) go through ImplicitGlobalGrid, which either passes device buffers to a CUDA-aware MPI (<code>IGG_CUDAAWARE_MPI=1</code>) or stages them through host memory (<code>=0</code>). <code>jupiter_env.sh</code> stages them: on the GH200 nodes the copy to the host is cheap, and with ParaStation MPI a <code>512^3</code> TGV took 64.5 against 75.2 ms per step on 4 GPUs and 41.7 against 79.8 ms on 8 GPUs of two nodes. On one node <code>mpi/halo_profile.jl</code> finds <code>update_halo!</code> slower with device buffers at every face size of that run (239 against 227 µs per call at the 530 KB faces of <code>512^3</code>, 542 against 152 µs at 134 KB, 212 against 136 µs at 9 KB), although MPI alone moves a 530 KB face between two GPUs of a node about 3 times faster from device buffers (<code>mpi/cuda_mpi_test.jl</code>). ImplicitGlobalGrid can also choose per direction (<code>IGG_CUDAAWARE_MPI_DIMX</code>, <code>_DIMY</code>, <code>_DIMZ</code>), but do not stage x while passing another direction as device buffers: ImplicitGlobalGrid 0.17.3 then never exchanges the x halos. With x and y on device buffers and z, the direction across the nodes (<code>srun --distribution=cyclic</code>), staged, 8 GPUs took 63.7 against 51.2 ms. <code>ForwardDiff.Dual</code> and <code>Bool</code> arrays, which ImplicitGlobalGrid does not take, go through WaterLily&#39;s own <code>MPI.Sendrecv!</code> on device buffers, so they need a CUDA-aware MPI either way.</p><h2 id="Scaling-curves" tabindex="-1">Scaling curves <a class="header-anchor" href="#Scaling-curves" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Scaling curves {#Scaling-curves}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>mpi/jobs/submit.sh</code> submits one job per node count, each running <code>mpi/jobs/&lt;cluster&gt;_scaling.sh</code>:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">bash</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/jobs/submit.sh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> jupiter</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> strong</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> tgv</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 9</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;1 2 4 8 16&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -A</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">budge</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">t</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">bash</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/jobs/submit.sh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> jupiter</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> weak</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> tgv</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 8</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;1 2 4 8 16 32&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -A</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">budge</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">t</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">bash</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/jobs/submit.sh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> jupiter_cpu</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> strong</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> tgv</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 8</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;1 2 4 8 16&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> -A</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">budge</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">t</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">bash</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/jobs/submit.sh</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> snellius</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> weak</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> tgv</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> 6</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;1 2 4 8&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The job on one node also runs the points inside it (on JUPITER 1, 2 and 4 GPUs, the first without MPI, which is the reference of the efficiency); the others run one point each, so a point that failed can be submitted again alone (<code>... weak tgv 6 &quot;32&quot; -- -A &lt;budget&gt;</code>). Each GPU job prints the cores of the ranks of its first node, which should be those of one Grace CPU each (0-71, 72-143, ...). The outputs go to <code>data/mpi/&lt;cluster&gt;_&lt;mode&gt;_&lt;case&gt;_&lt;log2p&gt;/</code>, and the plot scripts read a whole directory and save the figure in it:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=plotting</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/plot_strong.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> data/mpi/jupiter_strong_tgv_9</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">julia</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --project=plotting</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> mpi/plot_weak.jl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> data/mpi/jupiter_weak_tgv_8</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --refnp=4</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6A737D;--shiki-dark:#6A737D;">   # efficiency against one full node</span></span></code></pre></div><p>On Snellius, <code>WATERLILY_RPN</code> sets the ranks per node (default 64), spread evenly over the CCXs of each node. On JUPITER there is one rank per GPU, 4 per node. <code>jupiter_cpu</code> runs <code>mpi/jobs/jupiter_cpu_scaling.sh</code> on the CPUs of the JUPITER nodes (4 Grace CPUs of 72 cores): one thread per rank, <code>WATERLILY_RPN</code> ranks per node (default 256, which splits the <code>2^p</code> grids, unlike all 288 cores), the same number on each Grace CPU, spread evenly over its cores. The job prints the NUMA nodes of the first node, to check that the cores of each Grace CPU are numbered as the binding assumes.</p>`,24)])])}const g=s(n,[["render",o]]);export{k as __pageData,g as default};
